 \section{Conclusion}
-Conventional SAM spends a finite perturbation budget across all coordinates even though an N:M deletion acts only on the removed support. APBR-SAM addresses this mismatch by reallocating the same budget across coordinates and keeping the learned allocation as a persistent geometry. It then uses those scales to express weight deletion in normalized coordinates. Groupwise ranking minimizes the resulting deletion cost and, under the stated shared-curvature condition, its common loss-increment upper bound. At pruning time, conditional selection makes later decisions aware of earlier virtual deletions, and a single physical joint OBS update compensates the selected set. The current results support this integrated training-to-pruning path as an exploratory endpoint, while the isolated contribution of the geometric tie-break remains to be established by matched multi-seed controls.
-
-\end{samepage}
+APBR-SAM carries information from dense optimization into structured support selection. Global energy feedback learns persistent coordinate scales under a fixed perturbation budget. At pruning time, those scales determine the first deletion in each quartet; the second decision uses the reconstruction state induced by a virtual OBS update. Joint compensation then reconstructs the layer for the selected support. This division of roles gives the training geometry a direct entry point into pruning while using activation correlations to complete and compensate the decision. The resulting method connects historical perturbation allocation, conditional support selection, and second-order reconstruction in a single post-training 2:4 pipeline.
 
